14:31:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a completely fair way to look at it. If you strip away all the engineering jargon, you are 100% right: both machines have the exact same goal of taking colored liquid and putting it onto paper to make a copy. If we look at it from that zoomed-out perspective—that they are fundamentally doing the same job—then the best way to explain the crazy price difference is to look at **how the liquid is delivered**. Think about painting a wall. **The Newspaper Way (The Paint Roller):** Imagine painting a wall using a giant paint roller. You can buy a massive bucket of thick, cheap house paint for $20. You dip the roller in, slop it on the wall, and you're done. It’s fast, it’s messy, and the paint doesn't need to be fancy—it just needs to be colored liquid. **The Home Printer Way (The Fine-Art Airbrush):** Now imagine painting that exact same wall, but using a tiny, precision airbrush pen. You are still just "putting paint on a wall." But you can't use the cheap bucket of house paint anymore. If you put thick house paint into an airbrush, it will instantly clog the tiny needle and break the tool. To use the airbrush, you have to buy highly filtered, specially formulated, ultra-thin paint that costs $10 for a tiny little bottle. Both methods are just "putting paint on a wall." But the tool you are using forces you to buy a different *type* of paint. Your home printer is the airbrush. It works by firing ink through thousands of microscopic holes that are smaller than a human hair. If you put cheap newspaper ink into a home printer, it would clog and break instantly. When you buy a printer cartridge, you aren't really paying for the "color." You are paying for the extreme chemical engineering required to make a liquid that can shoot through a microscopic hole thousands of times a second without jamming. **But there is one more thing...** Even with that explanation, your frustration is totally justified, because **you are still being overcharged